QUESTION_PROMPT_BEGIN bytes=210
Find the sum of all integer bases $b>9$ for which $17_b$ is a divisor of $97_b.$

Solve the problem. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <integer>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=683
We are told that in base \(b\), the number \(17_b\) divides \(97_b\).  
Interpretation:  
\(17_b\) means \(1\cdot b + 7 = b+7\).  
\(97_b\) means \(9\cdot b + 7 = 9b+7\).  

We need integer bases \(b > 9\) such that  
\[
b+7 \mid 9b+7.
\]

We can use modular arithmetic:  
\(9b+7 \mod (b+7)\).  
Write \(9b+7 = 9(b+7) - 56\).  
So \(b+7 \mid 9b+7\) means \(b+7 \mid -56\), i.e., \(b+7 \mid 56\).

Thus \(b+7\) must be a positive divisor of 56. Since \(b > 9\), \(b+7 > 16\).  
Divisors of 56: 1, 2, 4, 7, 8, 14, 28, 56.  
Those greater than 16 are 28 and 56.  

So \(b+7 = 28 \Rightarrow b = 21\).  
\(b+7 = 56 \Rightarrow b = 49\).

Both are > 9. Sum = \(21 + 49 = 70\).

Answer: 70
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

