QUESTION_PROMPT_BEGIN bytes=580
The universe is filled with the Cosmic Microwave Background. Consider the annihilation of high energy \gamma-rays with a photon from the CMB Radiation into electron-positron, i.e. $\gamma\gamma\rightarrow e^{+}e^{-}$. From what energy \gamma-rays would have their lifetimes in the universe limited by this process? Knowing that the average photon energy of the CMB is $10^{-3}eV$.

Choices:
A. 9.5*1e4 GeV
B. 2.6*1e5 GeV
C. 1.8*1e5 GeV
D. 3.9*1e5 GeV

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=890
We need the threshold energy for pair production:  
A photon of energy \(E_\gamma\) collides with a CMB photon of average energy \(\epsilon \approx 10^{-3} \, \text{eV}\).  
The threshold condition for \(\gamma\gamma \to e^+e^-\) is  
\[
E_\gamma \epsilon \gtrsim (m_e c^2)^2
\]  
More precisely, for head-on collision, the invariant energy squared is \(4E_\gamma \epsilon\) (since both photons have negligible mass). The threshold is  
\[
4E_\gamma \epsilon \ge (2 m_e c^2)^2 = 4 (m_e c^2)^2
\]  
Thus  
\[
E_\gamma \ge \frac{(m_e c^2)^2}{\epsilon}
\]  
With \(m_e c^2 = 0.511 \, \text{MeV} = 5.11 \times 10^5 \, \text{eV}\),  
\[
E_\gamma \ge \frac{(5.11\times 10^5)^2}{10^{-3}} \, \text{eV} = \frac{2.61\times 10^{11}}{10^{-3}} = 2.61\times 10^{14} \, \text{eV}
\]  
Convert to GeV: \(2.61\times 10^{14} \, \text{eV} = 2.61\times 10^5 \, \text{GeV}\).  
That matches choice B.

Answer: B
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

