We need to compute throughput for different window sizes.  
Given:  
- Frame length = 512 bytes = 4096 bits  
- Data rate = 64 kb/s  
- Propagation delay = 270 ms  
- Acknowledgment is very short (negligible transmission time).  

First, transmission time for one frame:  
\( t_{tx} = \frac{4096 \text{ bits}}{64{,}000 \text{ b/s}} = 0.064 \text{ s} = 64 \text{ ms} \)  

Round-trip time (RTT) = 2 × propagation delay = 540 ms.  

For stop-and-wait (window size = 1):  
Throughput = \(\frac{\text{frame size}}{\text{total cycle time}}\)  
Total cycle time = \( t_{tx} + \text{RTT} = 64 + 540 = 604 \text{ ms} \)  
Throughput = \(\frac{4096}{0.604} \approx 6781.5 \text{ b/s} \approx 6.8 \text{ kb/s}\)  

For window size = 7:  
We can send up to 7 frames before waiting for first ACK.  
Time to send 7 frames = \(7 \times 64 = 448 \text{ ms}\).  
Since RTT = 540 ms, the ACK for first frame arrives after 540 ms, so we can keep sending continuously if window is large enough.  
Here, 448 ms < 540 ms, so there is idle time.  
Total time for 7 frames = 64 ms (first frame) + 540 ms (wait for ACK) = 604 ms, but during that wait we sent 6 more frames? Actually careful:  
We send first frame at t=0, finish at 64 ms. ACK arrives at 64+540=604 ms.  
We can send frames 2–7 during the waiting period. The last frame (7th) finishes at 64×7=448 ms. Then we wait until 604 ms for ACK of first frame. So total time for 7 frames = 604 ms.  
Throughput = \(\frac{7 \times 4096}{0.604} \approx 47483 \text{ b/s} \approx 47.5 \text{ kb/s}\)  

For window size = 17:  
Time to send 17 frames = \(17 \times 64 = 1088 \text{ ms}\).  
RTT = 540 ms. Since 1088 > 540, we can send continuously without idle time.  
Maximum throughput = data rate = 64 kb/s.  
But careful: The channel is fully utilized, so throughput = 64 kb/s.  

For window size = 117:  
Even larger, so also 64 kb/s.  

Thus results: 6.8, 47.5, 64, 64 kb/s.  
That matches option C.  

Answer: C
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

