If each revolution gives two crossings (in a cyclotron, typically one gap crossed twice per revolution? Actually in a classical cyclotron, the particle crosses the gap twice per revolution, gaining energy each time. In a synchrocyclotron, it might be similar. So each revolution gives two accelerations. The problem says "undergoes two accelerations" to reach 950 MeV. That might mean after two accelerations (i.e., one revolution) it reaches 950 MeV? That would imply gain per acceleration ~475 MeV, but U0 is only 0.19 MV, impossible. So "two accelerations" likely means the process involves two stages? Or maybe it's a trick: The proton is injected and then accelerated twice (maybe two separate gaps) to reach that energy, but the number of revolutions is asked.

