QUESTION_PROMPT_BEGIN bytes=1017
The lactation yield of Shanghai cows is 12% higher than that of Guenseys cows, and the butterfat content of Guenseys cows is 30% higher than that of Shanghai cows. The difference in lactation yield and butterfat content consists of about 10 pairs of genes each, which is a microallelic polygenic gene. The difference in lactation and butterfat is about 10 pairs of genes each, which is a microallelic polygenic gene. Now, if we cross Shanghai cows with Guenseys, what percentage of individuals in the F2 will have the same high lactation and butterfat content as Shanghai cows?

Choices:
A. ${9.091 \times 10^{-13} }$
B. ${6.298 \times 10^{-12} }$
C. ${9.390 \times 10^{-13} }$
D. ${9.195 \times 10^{-14} }$
E. ${7.543 \times 10^{-12} }$
F. ${9.295 \times 10^{-13} }$
G. ${8.293 \times 10^{-13} }$
H. ${8.456 \times 10^{-14} }$
I. ${9.095 \times 10^{-13} }$
J. ${8.095 \times 10^{-12} }$

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=867
We are told that the difference in lactation yield and butterfat content each involves about 10 pairs of genes (microallelic polygenic). That means each trait is controlled by 10 independently segregating loci, each with additive alleles.  

For a cross between two pure lines differing in 10 pairs of genes, the F1 is heterozygous at all 10 loci. In the F2, the probability of an individual being homozygous for the "Shanghai" alleles at all 10 loci for one trait is \((1/4)^{10}\). Since the two traits are independent (different sets of 10 loci), the probability for both traits is \((1/4)^{10} \times (1/4)^{10} = (1/4)^{20}\).

Compute \((1/4)^{20} = 1 / 4^{20}\).  
\(4^{10} = 1,048,576\), so \(4^{20} = (1,048,576)^2 = 1,099,511,627,776\).  
Thus probability = \(1 / 1,099,511,627,776 \approx 9.095 \times 10^{-13}\).

That matches choice I exactly.

Answer: I
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

