Assuming the signal $x(t)=(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t})^2$ has a Fourier transform result of $X(j\omega)$, with a sampling frequency $\omega_s=150\pi$, the sampled signal is $g(t)$, and the Fourier transform of $g(t)$ is $G(j\omega)$. If $G(j\omega)$ is required to satisfy: $G(j\omega)=75X(j\omega) \quad for \quad |\omega|\leq\omega_0$, what is the maximum value of $\omega_0$?

Choices:
A. 50\pi
B. 37.5\pi
C. 150\pi
D. 125\pi
E. 43.75\pi
F. 62.5\pi
G. 75\pi
H. 25\pi
I. 87.5\pi
J. 100\pi

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=2148
We have  
\[
x(t) = \left( \frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t} \right)^2
\]  
The Fourier transform of \(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t}\) is a rectangle of height 1 from \(-50\pi\) to \(50\pi\). Squaring in time corresponds to convolution in frequency: the convolution of two rectangles of width \(100\pi\) gives a triangle of width \(200\pi\) centered at 0, with maximum height \(100\pi\) (area of rectangle). So  
\[
X(j\omega) = \text{tri}\left( \frac{\omega}{100\pi} \right) \cdot \frac{1}{2\pi}? 
\]  
Actually careful:  
\(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t} \leftrightarrow \text{rect}(\omega/(100\pi))\) with height 1.  
Then squaring in time: \(x(t) = [\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t}]^2\) corresponds to \((1/(2\pi))\) times convolution of that rect with itself. Convolution of two rects of width \(100\pi\) gives a triangle from \(-100\pi\) to \(100\pi\) with peak \(100\pi\). Scaling:  
\[
X(j\omega) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \cdot \text{tri}\left( \frac{\omega}{100\pi} \right) \cdot 100\pi = 50 \cdot \text{tri}\left( \frac{\omega}{100\pi} \right)
\]  
So \(X(j\omega)\) is a triangle:  
- zero for \(|\omega| > 100\pi\)  
- peak \(50\) at \(\omega=0\)  
- linearly decreasing to 0 at \(\omega = \pm 100\pi\).

Sampling frequency \(\omega_s = 150\pi\), so sampling period \(T = 2\pi / \omega_s = 2\pi/(150\pi) = 1/75\).  
Sampled signal \(g(t) = x(t) \cdot \sum \delta(t - nT)\). Its Fourier transform \(G(j\omega) = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} X(j(\omega - k\omega_s))\).  
Here \(1/T = 75\). So  
\[
G(j\omega) = 75 \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} X(j(\omega - 150\pi k)).
\]

We want \(G(j\omega) = 75 X(j\omega)\) for \(|\omega| \leq \omega_0\). That means in that range, there must be no overlap from shifted copies. The baseband copy is centered at 0, extends to \(100\pi\). The first positive copy is centered at \(150\pi\), extends from \(50\pi\) to \(250\pi\). So the baseband copy is unaltered only up to the point where the first positive copy starts: that is at \(\omega = 150\pi - 100\pi = 50\pi\). Similarly negative copy starts at \(-50\pi\). So maximum \(\omega_0\) without aliasing is \(50\pi\).

Thus answer: A. 50\pi.

Final line:  
Answer: A
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

